<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Document</title>
</head>
<body>
    <script>
     function getUser(id, cb){
        setTimeout(()=>{
            cb(`User ${id}`)
        }, 500)
     }

     function getPosts(userId,cb){
        setTimeout(()=>{
            cb(`Posts for ${userId}`)
        }, 500)
     }

     function getComments(postId,cb){
        setTimeout(()=>{
            cb(`Comments for ${postId}`)
        }, 500)
     }
   
     getUser(1, (User)=>{
        console.log(User)
        getPosts(User, (Posts)=>{
            console.log(Posts)
            getComments(Posts, (Comments)=>{
                console.log(Comments)
            })
        })
     })

    </script>    

</body>
</html>
<!---- Write three functions: getUser(id, cb), getPosts(userId, cb), getComments(postId, cb) —
 each fakes a 500ms delay then returns dummy data. Chain them the ugly way: 
nested callbacks 3 levels deep. 
This is intentional pain — you need to feel it before Promises make sense.--->
